1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
Ever used a navigator? Going somewhere
new, you stuck it on the windshield.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:09,730
The company that sold the most
of them worldwide is Garmin.

3
00:00:10,767 --> 00:00:16,467
In 2008, over 70% of its revenue came
from car and portable navigation.

4
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:21,177
Then phones got free navigation.
All said: Garmin's crushed.

5
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:26,607
But guess what: its fiscal 2025
revenue was over twice that year's.

6
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:31,453
Stranger still: hunted by
phones, it made a phone itself.

7
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:37,180
And in its annual report to regulators,
it wrote: our growth has stopped.

8
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:41,726
Today's question: a company
everyone says phones crushed,

9
00:00:41,726 --> 00:00:44,417
so how did its revenue double?

10
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:49,057
I'm a Curio Files researcher;
let's start at the top.

11
00:00:49,057 --> 00:00:53,167
Our first lead, Min Kao,
was born in Zhushan, Taiwan.

12
00:00:53,167 --> 00:00:59,167
In 1971 he finished EE at NTU,
then earned an EE PhD at Tennessee.

13
00:00:59,167 --> 00:01:01,987
He built navigation systems in the US,

14
00:01:01,987 --> 00:01:05,197
then satellite military
navigation at a defense firm.

15
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:08,836
Then, satellite positioning
was a brand-new system.

16
00:01:08,836 --> 00:01:11,480
Who'd guess it would reach your car?

17
00:01:13,467 --> 00:01:18,577
Our second lead, Gary Burrell, ran several
aircraft and marine electronics firms.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,721
Garmin says he designed
the first successful

19
00:01:22,721 --> 00:01:25,150
nav/comm for small private planes.

20
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,593
In 1983, Min Kao also
joined an avionics company,

21
00:01:30,593 --> 00:01:33,667
and the two became colleagues. And then?

22
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:38,367
In 1989 they started their
own company, at first ProNav.

23
00:01:38,367 --> 00:01:41,907
Later renamed Garmin: Gary
and Min, put together.

24
00:01:42,267 --> 00:01:45,187
Its first products: for planes,

25
00:01:45,233 --> 00:01:46,583
and for boaters.

26
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:50,623
Notice something? It still had
nothing for your family car.

27
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:54,433
So how did it get in?

28
00:01:54,433 --> 00:01:59,943
Per Wikipedia, its first portable
car navigator came only in 1998.

29
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:03,730
Problem was, satellite
signals had a big flaw.

30
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:09,867
The US deliberately added error to the
civilian signal, citing national security.

31
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:15,787
On May 1, 2000, the White House said
the error would end at midnight.

32
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:20,173
It said civilian accuracy
could get up to 10x better.

33
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:24,933
For a machine telling you to turn
left here, I'd say that's huge.

34
00:02:26,267 --> 00:02:30,159
Six years later, in
2006, business exploded:

35
00:02:30,159 --> 00:02:33,800
company revenue jumped 73% in one go.

36
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,233
And cars?

37
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:37,600
Up 170%.

38
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:39,800
The star was a line called nüvi.

39
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:47,233
Auto was 28% of revenue in 2006, 52%
the next year, 64% the year after.

40
00:02:47,233 --> 00:02:52,433
Picture it: of every $3, nearly
$2 from one kind of device.

41
00:02:52,433 --> 00:02:55,155
It called its own factories in Xizhi,

42
00:02:55,155 --> 00:02:58,063
Zhongli and Linkou,
Taiwan, a core strength.

43
00:02:59,267 --> 00:03:02,647
Selling like that,
profits must have soared?

44
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:09,257
Nope. The year nüvi hit 64%,
it shipped 38% more units.

45
00:03:10,767 --> 00:03:13,387
Revenue rose just 10%.

46
00:03:13,467 --> 00:03:19,637
The extra volume got eaten by price. It
wrote: prices fell sharply over two years.

47
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:22,597
Let me lay out that year's books.

48
00:03:23,367 --> 00:03:26,414
Of every $100 in car navigation,

49
00:03:26,414 --> 00:03:31,167
it kept just $38.50 after
costs; aviation kept $67.

50
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:36,177
So its best-selling navigator was
its least profitable business.

51
00:03:36,733 --> 00:03:39,350
Revenue fell 14% that last quarter;

52
00:03:39,350 --> 00:03:42,803
it blamed the economy
and the financial crisis.

53
00:03:43,533 --> 00:03:47,216
US-listed firms file an
annual report each year with

54
00:03:47,216 --> 00:03:50,693
the US securities regulator,
on business and risks.

55
00:03:51,167 --> 00:03:56,067
In February 2009, what did
Garmin's report say under risks?

56
00:03:56,067 --> 00:04:01,367
It said: if phone navigation catches
on, it could slow our growth.

57
00:04:01,367 --> 00:04:04,267
Phones were coming. Its plan?

58
00:04:04,467 --> 00:04:07,737
Its answer was blunt. Remember that phone?

59
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:13,980
The same month, it announced an alliance
with Taiwan's ASUS to co-brand phones.

60
00:04:14,967 --> 00:04:20,933
Unveiled a year earlier, nüvifone
had calls, a browser, and navigation.

61
00:04:20,933 --> 00:04:25,903
See the move? If phones will steal
navigation, make the phone yourself.

62
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:31,110
Honestly, at first I thought: clever.

63
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:32,834
It promised the first half;

64
00:04:32,834 --> 00:04:36,407
the first model only went
on sale in Taiwan that July.

65
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:43,170
One thing differed: this phone wasn't made
in its own factories but by someone else.

66
00:04:44,467 --> 00:04:47,527
In October, a big US carrier signed on.

67
00:04:47,833 --> 00:04:52,013
But on the 28th that month, another
company posted a blog entry.

68
00:04:53,933 --> 00:04:58,773
It was Google: Google Maps
Navigation, for Android 2.0 phones.

69
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:04,363
It had what a navigator has: voice
guidance, 3D view, auto rerouting.

70
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:08,933
And: never buy map updates again.

71
00:05:08,933 --> 00:05:10,293
The price?

72
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:16,250
Free. BBC noted rival phone
navigation apps cost up to £60.

73
00:05:16,633 --> 00:05:21,963
That day Garmin's stock fell
about 17%; BBC reported 18%.

74
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:25,600
Holding its shares, you'd hardly sleep.

75
00:05:26,133 --> 00:05:31,083
A famous tech blogger wrote that day:
dedicated navigators are nearly done.

76
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:36,520
A finance site's headline asked
if this was a category killer.

77
00:05:37,867 --> 00:05:42,137
I think "phones crushed navigators"
began right about that day.

78
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:45,130
Maybe you took yours down then.

79
00:05:46,133 --> 00:05:50,543
Four months later came the next
annual report. Did "could" change?

80
00:05:52,767 --> 00:05:54,547
No. Still "could."

81
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:59,120
It also said several upcoming
phones would run Google's Android.

82
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:07,867
It tallied the prior year too:
revenue down over 15%, auto down 19%.

83
00:06:07,867 --> 00:06:11,587
But note: auto units fell only 2%.

84
00:06:12,667 --> 00:06:17,107
What fell was price: the average down 18%.

85
00:06:17,433 --> 00:06:20,903
Aviation and marine fell
too; it cited the economy.

86
00:06:21,733 --> 00:06:24,263
For auto, it cited competition.

87
00:06:24,567 --> 00:06:29,247
Free navigation came near year's end,
so not all that drop is on phones.

88
00:06:30,267 --> 00:06:32,707
When would "could" finally change?

89
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:35,410
First, it gave up on its phone.

90
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:41,119
In October 2010, it and ASUS
said no new co-branded phones;

91
00:06:41,119 --> 00:06:43,280
the report just said: cancelled.

92
00:06:44,367 --> 00:06:49,700
That year auto revenue fell
19% again, under $1.7 billion.

93
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:54,150
As boss, with 60% of income
sliding, what would you write?

94
00:06:54,733 --> 00:06:58,733
On February 23, 2011, the report went in.

95
00:06:58,733 --> 00:07:02,143
Remember "our growth has
stopped"? This was it.

96
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:09,400
In the same sentence, "could slow"
became "has caused our growth to stop."

97
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:13,070
The next part added:
this market has peaked.

98
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:17,443
Note: its own account. The
same report says more new

99
00:07:17,443 --> 00:07:21,253
cars offer navigation, and
lists the economy as a risk.

100
00:07:21,933 --> 00:07:23,703
The known ending?

101
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:28,030
But that same day it also
put out an earnings release.

102
00:07:28,133 --> 00:07:33,963
Its headline skipped navigators: outdoor,
fitness, aviation and marine stay strong.

103
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:38,473
They earned 61% of operating income.

104
00:07:39,267 --> 00:07:41,707
Yet just 38% of revenue.

105
00:07:41,967 --> 00:07:47,077
Even counting deferred revenue back in,
by my math they still earned over half.

106
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:50,284
They didn't suddenly surge.

107
00:07:50,284 --> 00:07:56,600
Mainly auto's operating income fell
from $520M to $250M in two years.

108
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:00,832
Its segments then: auto was
biggest, plus three small ones:

109
00:08:00,832 --> 00:08:03,490
outdoor and fitness, marine, and aviation.

110
00:08:04,633 --> 00:08:07,723
So when did those three
small businesses start?

111
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,950
Answer: always there. Remember
those planes and boats?

112
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:16,674
Back to 2003: Cessna picked its integrated

113
00:08:16,674 --> 00:08:19,830
avionics as standard
on a new business jet.

114
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:25,661
That November it began shipping a
runner's device that measured speed,

115
00:08:25,661 --> 00:08:27,650
distance and laps by satellite.

116
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:33,433
In 2003, your phone was
maybe running Snake.

117
00:08:33,433 --> 00:08:36,813
So I don't call it a
desperate pivot. My view:

118
00:08:37,967 --> 00:08:41,677
A phone rides along, but you
won't wear it for a marathon,

119
00:08:42,733 --> 00:08:44,967
or wire it into a jet.

120
00:08:44,967 --> 00:08:51,733
Then the numbers moved: fitness
grew 42% in 2010, 24% the next year.

121
00:08:51,733 --> 00:08:57,083
In July 2012, it launched its
first GPS watch for hikers: fēnix.

122
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:03,267
List price $399. Would you buy one?

123
00:09:03,267 --> 00:09:04,837
Not me. I don't climb.

124
00:09:05,467 --> 00:09:07,800
But it's no comeback yet.

125
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:13,000
Company revenue stalled, down
to about $2.6 billion in 2013.

126
00:09:13,333 --> 00:09:17,943
Auto's share of revenue
fell from 58% to 19%.

127
00:09:18,233 --> 00:09:25,200
It topped its fiscal 2008 peak only
in fiscal 2019, near $3.8 billion.

128
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:27,133
Eleven years.

129
00:09:27,133 --> 00:09:30,323
So where's that 70% business now?

130
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,519
Remember "over twice"?

131
00:09:32,519 --> 00:09:38,870
In fiscal 2025's $7.2 billion-plus,
there's no piece called that 70%.

132
00:09:39,433 --> 00:09:45,828
Fitness, nearly a third; outdoor 28%;
marine and aviation over 10% each.

133
00:09:45,828 --> 00:09:48,533
The smallest piece, under 10%.

134
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:50,450
The story's not over.

135
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:56,000
You may think: 70% down to under
10%, that's how auto vanished.

136
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:02,060
The smallest supplies automakers: under
10%, and still losing money for the year.

137
00:10:02,167 --> 00:10:06,367
It makes systems built into new
cars, not the old business renamed.

138
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:09,780
And that windshield gadget?

139
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:14,780
Still sold; filed under outdoor,
with no separate figure.

140
00:10:15,167 --> 00:10:19,937
Back around 2017: phone companies
kept coming after fitness, too.

141
00:10:21,433 --> 00:10:28,073
The fiscal 2018 report lists fitness
rivals: Apple, Samsung, Huawei.

142
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,170
See? Several sell phones. The same
rivals followed you to your wrist.

143
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:38,317
It admitted that the year before, 2017,

144
00:10:38,317 --> 00:10:43,100
fitness revenue fell 7%, blaming
a shrinking basic band market.

145
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:44,510
Crushed again?

146
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:50,770
Then in 2018 fitness grew 13%
to become its biggest segment.

147
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:53,967
Beat Apple? No.

148
00:10:53,967 --> 00:10:57,067
Counterpoint's Q1 2022 figures:

149
00:10:57,433 --> 00:11:01,133
Apple had 36% of shipments; Garmin, fifth.

150
00:11:01,133 --> 00:11:04,583
But above $500, Garmin ranked first.

151
00:11:05,233 --> 00:11:07,573
Just one quarter, not forever.

152
00:11:08,233 --> 00:11:13,943
Latest: Q3 results out this October
28, revenue nearly $2 billion.

153
00:11:14,767 --> 00:11:16,707
Does that date ring a bell?

154
00:11:17,267 --> 00:11:21,457
Seventeen years ago, that free
navigation post went up that day.

155
00:11:22,733 --> 00:11:27,163
Its own forecast: over
$8.1B. Not yet real.

156
00:11:27,533 --> 00:11:31,300
So did Garmin ever beat
phones at navigators?

157
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:35,830
No. Phones stopped its growth:
its own words, in its report.

158
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:39,555
But per its own reports,
phones weren't alone:

159
00:11:39,555 --> 00:11:42,970
built-in car nav, the
economy and price wars too.

160
00:11:43,833 --> 00:11:47,435
Revenue doubled on
business beyond the car:

161
00:11:47,435 --> 00:11:50,913
cockpits, boats, runners, and your wrist.

162
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:55,105
My guess: had it bet
only on car navigation,

163
00:11:55,105 --> 00:11:58,420
this file would likely
stop at the 2011 page.

164
00:11:58,667 --> 00:12:00,547
And that suction-cup spot?

165
00:12:01,133 --> 00:12:04,033
Per its reports, phones
did take a big chunk.

166
00:12:05,333 --> 00:12:09,212
But I think this file says: what
got crushed was one business;

167
00:12:09,212 --> 00:12:13,323
what held the company up was the
others it had started long before.

168
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:17,240
That's today's story.

169
00:12:17,833 --> 00:12:21,093
Still got a navigator on your
windshield? Tell me below.

170
00:12:22,533 --> 00:12:27,273
This channel covers only what's on
record. To dig with us, subscribe.
